{"records":[{"id":"6rdzdjp624","title":"KrasG12D/+ co-mutation alters the landscape of lung adenocarcinoma","description":"Tumor pieces from Kras-mutant genetically engineered lung adenocarcinoma mouse models with loss Lkb1 and/or Keap1 were assessed. \nSteady-state LC-MS analysis (M03818) was performed on tumor pieces; or normal lung pieces. Location is annotated.\n13C,15N-Glutamine 15 minute pulse-labeling (M04050) of mice was performed at defined timepoints when tumor burden is known to be significantly progressed. Tumor pieces and plasma were isolated from n=5 mice/genotype.\nRelated to manuscript: \nBest et al., Glutaminase inhibition impairs CD8 T cell activation in STK11/Lkb1 deficient lung cancer. Cell Metabolism, 2022","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-04-21T17:35:47.282Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/6rdzdjp624","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/6rdzdjp624"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Sarah","last_name":"Best","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Kate","last_name":"Sutherland","orcid":null},{"first_name":"David ","last_name":"Pirman","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Joshua","last_name":"Goldford","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Gillian","last_name":"Kingsbury","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Katie","last_name":"Sellers","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yashira","last_name":"Negrón Abril","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Metabolomics","15Nitrogen Labeling"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"4wwcjg93gh","title":"LKB1inactivation elicits an NNMT-mediated methyl sink and confers dependence on PRMT5 in lung cancer","description":"The protein arginine methyl transferase 5 (PRMT5) emerges as a therapeutic target in S-methyl-5'-thioadenosine phosphorylase (MTAP)-deleted cancers, where MTA accumulation partially inhibits its  activity. However, It remains unclear whether other genetic alterations can dictate PRMT5 activity in cancer. Here we identify LKB1 as an alternative predictor of PRMT5 inhibition in lung cancer independent of MTAP. Mechanistically, LKB1 loss activates SIK1/2-CRTC2 signaling to upregulate NNMT, creating a \"methyl sink\" that lowers the SAM/SAH ratio and attenuates PRMT5 activity. NNMT overexpression is sufficient to induce this hypomorphic PRMT5 state and heighten sensitivity to PRMT5 inhibitors. Functionally, PRMT5 inhibition induces senescence in LKB1-deficient cells and confers vulnerability to navitoclax, synergistically blunting tumor growth in vivo. Collectively, we identify PRMT5 as an actionable therapeutic vulnerability in LKB1-deficient lung cancer, and propose LKB1 status/NNMT expression as potential biomarkers for PRMT5 inhibition. These findings may expand the clinical utility of PRMT5-targeted therapies beyond MTAP-deleted cancers. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-05-01T11:15:27.93Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/4wwcjg93gh","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/4wwcjg93gh"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"WEN","last_name":"MI","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"wpw7mstpp8","title":"Gene sequencing of a rare mutant lung cancer","description":"This sequencing contains 73 genes associated with the occurrence and development of lung cancer, including important genes related to targeted therapy and drug resistance such as BRAF, ALK, RET, ROS1, MET, EGFR, KRAS, ERBB2 in the NCCN guidelines, as well as genes related to PD-1/PD-L1 immunotherapy such as MLH1, MSH2, MSH6, PMS2, POLE, and genes related to chemotherapy drug toxicity and efficacy.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-09-01T15:15:22.885Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/wpw7mstpp8","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/wpw7mstpp8"],"contributors":[{"first_name":" FANGFANG ","last_name":"WANG","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Gene Sequencing"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"k9sm56gj5y","title":"cisplatin-damaged gene locus-replicate 1-Blank-R1","description":"Cisplatin is a DNA-targeting chemotherapeutic, yet its damaged gene loci are largely unexplored. We utilized a forward chemical genetics strategy to map 7578 cisplatin-damaged genes (CDGs) with >12 fold-enrichment from A549 human lung cancer cells, of which the highly associated signalling pathways include sperm motility, molecular mechanism of cancer, and protein kinase A signalling. The three most frequently cisplatinated penta-nucleotide motifs and genomic region are CTGGG, CCAGG and GCTGG, and promoter, respectively. Among the CDGs are 1330 enzyme, 747 transcription regulator and 486 transporter genes. Importantly, cisplatin targeted 306 protein kinase genes, accounting for 59% of putative protein kinase genes in the human genome, and 92 protein phosphatase genes. This indicates that cisplatin genome-wide disturbs protein phosphorylation. This data set contains the original sequencing data of two biological replicates, matching results to the hg38 genome, and the analysis results of the distribution of the damage areas identified by the two sequencing results.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-06-23T16:23:56.067Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/k9sm56gj5y","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/k9sm56gj5y"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Fuyi","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cancer","Gene"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"s7jbxvwf4z","title":"cisplatin-damaged gene locus-replicate 2-L-R2","description":"Cisplatin is a DNA-targeting chemotherapeutic, yet its damaged gene loci are largely unexplored. We utilized a forward chemical genetics strategy to map 7578 cisplatin-damaged genes (CDGs) with >12 fold-enrichment from A549 human lung cancer cells, of which the highly associated signalling pathways include sperm motility, molecular mechanism of cancer, and protein kinase A signalling. The three most frequently cisplatinated penta-nucleotide motifs and genomic region are CTGGG, CCAGG and GCTGG, and promoter, respectively. Among the CDGs are 1330 enzyme, 747 transcription regulator and 486 transporter genes. Importantly, cisplatin targeted 306 protein kinase genes, accounting for 59% of putative protein kinase genes in the human genome, and 92 protein phosphatase genes. This indicates that cisplatin genome-wide disturbs protein phosphorylation. This data set contains the original sequencing data of two biological replicates, matching results to the hg38 genome, and the analysis results of the distribution of the damage areas identified by the two sequencing results.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-06-23T16:31:49.088Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/s7jbxvwf4z","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/s7jbxvwf4z"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Fuyi","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cancer","Gene"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"fv6f9rpg5m","title":"cisplatin-damaged gene locus-replicate 2-L-R1","description":"Cisplatin is a DNA-targeting chemotherapeutic, yet its damaged gene loci are largely unexplored. We utilized a forward chemical genetics strategy to map 7578 cisplatin-damaged genes (CDGs) with >12 fold-enrichment from A549 human lung cancer cells, of which the highly associated signalling pathways include sperm motility, molecular mechanism of cancer, and protein kinase A signalling. The three most frequently cisplatinated penta-nucleotide motifs and genomic region are CTGGG, CCAGG and GCTGG, and promoter, respectively. Among the CDGs are 1330 enzyme, 747 transcription regulator and 486 transporter genes. Importantly, cisplatin targeted 306 protein kinase genes, accounting for 59% of putative protein kinase genes in the human genome, and 92 protein phosphatase genes. This indicates that cisplatin genome-wide disturbs protein phosphorylation. This data set contains the original sequencing data of two biological replicates, matching results to the hg38 genome, and the analysis results of the distribution of the damage areas identified by the two sequencing results.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-06-23T16:32:38.387Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/fv6f9rpg5m","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/fv6f9rpg5m"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Fuyi","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cancer","Gene"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"xb6s7vzcwm","title":"cisplatin-damaged gene locus-replicate 1-Sample1-R1","description":"Cisplatin is a DNA-targeting chemotherapeutic, yet its damaged gene loci are largely unexplored. We utilized a forward chemical genetics strategy to map 7578 cisplatin-damaged genes (CDGs) with >12 fold-enrichment from A549 human lung cancer cells, of which the highly associated signalling pathways include sperm motility, molecular mechanism of cancer, and protein kinase A signalling. The three most frequently cisplatinated penta-nucleotide motifs and genomic region are CTGGG, CCAGG and GCTGG, and promoter, respectively. Among the CDGs are 1330 enzyme, 747 transcription regulator and 486 transporter genes. Importantly, cisplatin targeted 306 protein kinase genes, accounting for 59% of putative protein kinase genes in the human genome, and 92 protein phosphatase genes. This indicates that cisplatin genome-wide disturbs protein phosphorylation. This data set contains the original sequencing data of two biological replicates, matching results to the hg38 genome, and the analysis results of the distribution of the damage areas identified by the two sequencing results.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-06-23T16:29:23.643Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/xb6s7vzcwm","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/xb6s7vzcwm"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Fuyi","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cancer","Gene"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"dw3v4sm9nb","title":"cisplatin-damaged gene locus-replicate 2-Blank-R2","description":"Cisplatin is a DNA-targeting chemotherapeutic, yet its damaged gene loci are largely unexplored. We utilized a forward chemical genetics strategy to map 7578 cisplatin-damaged genes (CDGs) with >12 fold-enrichment from A549 human lung cancer cells, of which the highly associated signalling pathways include sperm motility, molecular mechanism of cancer, and protein kinase A signalling. The three most frequently cisplatinated penta-nucleotide motifs and genomic region are CTGGG, CCAGG and GCTGG, and promoter, respectively. Among the CDGs are 1330 enzyme, 747 transcription regulator and 486 transporter genes. Importantly, cisplatin targeted 306 protein kinase genes, accounting for 59% of putative protein kinase genes in the human genome, and 92 protein phosphatase genes. This indicates that cisplatin genome-wide disturbs protein phosphorylation. This data set contains the original sequencing data of two biological replicates, matching results to the hg38 genome, and the analysis results of the distribution of the damage areas identified by the two sequencing results.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-06-23T16:26:36.327Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/dw3v4sm9nb","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/dw3v4sm9nb"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Fuyi","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cancer","Gene"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"2tv7ntcgh6","title":"c